WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.750
Have you ever wondered how your voice, or
your guitar would sound in the middle of a

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.110
space station?

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:17.640
A realistic simulation of sounds within virtual
environments dramatically improves the immersion

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.920
of the user in computer games and virtual
reality applications.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.099
To be able to simulate these effects, we need
to compute the interaction between sound waves

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:29.929
and the geometry and materials within the
scene.

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:35.329
If you remember, we also had quite a few episodes
about light simulations, where we simulated

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:40.570
the interaction of light rays or waves and
the scene we have at hand.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.390
Sounds quite similar, right?

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:48.280
Well, kind of, and the great thing is that
we can reuse quite a bit of this knowledge

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.320
and some of the equations for light transport
for sound.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.539
This technique we call path tracing, and it
is one of the many well-known techniques used

00:00:57.539 --> 00:00:59.110
for sound simulation.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:05.550
We can use path tracing to simulate the path
of many waves to obtain an impulse response,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:10.600
which is a simple mathematical function that
describes the reverberation that we hear if

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.610
we shoot a gun in a given scene, such as a
space station or a church.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:21.160
After we obtained these impulse reponses,
we can use an operation called the convolution

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.950
with our input signal, like our voice to get
a really convincing result.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.160
We have talked about this in more detail in
earlier episodes, I've put a link for them

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.050
in the video description box.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:38.820
It is important to know that the impulse reponse
depends on the scene and where we, the listeners

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.800
are exactly in the scene.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.270
In pretty much every concert ever, we find
that sound reverberations are quite different

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.090
in the middle of the arena versus standing
at the back.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.430
One of the main contributions of this work
is that it exploits temporal coherence.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:02:00.120
This means that even though the impulse response
is different if we stand at different places,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.700
but these locations don't change arbitrarily
and we can reuse a lot of information from

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.440
the previous few impulse reponses that we
worked so hard to compute.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:16.060
This way, we can get away with tracing much
fewer rays and still get high-quality results.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:21.520
In the best cases, the algorithm executes
five times as quickly as previous techniques,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.310
and the memory requirements are significantly
more favorable.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:28.000
The paper also contains a user study.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.200
Limitations include a bit overly smooth audio
signals and some fidelity loss in the lower

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.960
frequency domains.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.940
Some of these scenes in the footage showcase
up to 24 distinct sound sources, and all of

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:45.880
them are simulated against the geometry and
the materials found in the scene.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.040
So let's listen together and delight in these
magnificent results.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.489
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

